        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill good for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most older adults who walk steadily without a cane or walker and have no uncontrolled heart condition, yes. A treadmill gives a flat, predictable surface, removes weather from the equation and makes it easy to start slowly. The main risk is falling on or off a moving belt, so use the safety key and handrails and ask your doctor first if you have heart, balance or joint conditions.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is walking on a treadmill good for elderly people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, at gentle settings and with sensible precautions. Regular moderate walking supports heart and lung fitness, helps keep the legs working and contributes to the 150 minutes a week of moderate activity US guidelines recommend. People with recent falls, dizziness, memory problems or a balance condition should start under supervision or choose a seated machine instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the benefits of a treadmill for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The main benefits are year-round walking regardless of weather, a flat surface without kerbs or ice, precise control of speed in small steps, handrails within reach, and the ability to stop and sit down at home. Health benefits come from the walking itself: better aerobic fitness, and help maintaining a comfortable walking speed, which research links with better outcomes in older adults.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill or walking outside better for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Neither is better for everyone. A treadmill removes uneven ground and weather but adds a moving belt. Walking outside trains balance on varied ground and brings daylight and company, but kerbs, ice and distance from home are hazards. Many older adults do best with both: the treadmill on bad days, outdoor walks when conditions are good.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a safer alternative to a treadmill for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A recumbent bike or a seated stepper (recumbent cross-trainer) gives similar heart and leg work while you sit, with very little fall risk. A standing elliptical removes the moving belt but still needs standing balance. Supervised balance classes such as tai chi have good evidence for reducing falls.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>At what age should you stop using a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no age limit. What matters is whether you can walk steadily without a walking aid, use the stop button and safety key reliably, and have your doctor's agreement for any heart, balance or joint conditions. Signs it may be time to switch include drifting back on the belt, catching toes, frequent dizziness or a recent fall.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

